  <div class="slide caption-page" data-document-role="page" data-label="C6 - Caption (copy/paste)">
    <div class="caption-flag">Caption - not for posting</div>
    <div class="caption-body">There is a specific part of the brain, the insula, whose job is to sense what is happening inside the body. The process is called interoception.<br><br>Interoception is how we notice hunger, thirst, a full bladder, or a heart beating fast. Brain scans show the insula lighting up during these tasks even in children as young as six. But when those internal signals come through faintly, a child might not register that they are hungry until they are already falling apart.<br><br>So the meltdown that seems to come from nowhere at five in the evening may be a body that quietly missed its own hunger cue for hours. That is not carelessness or bad behavior. It is a brain sense that reads the body more softly than most. The body was simply speaking too quietly to hear.</div>
    <div class="caption-tags">#interoception #insula #bodysignals</div>
    <div class="caption-accounts">Tag: none needed</div>
