  <div class="slide caption-page" data-document-role="page" data-label="C7 - Caption (copy/paste)">
    <div class="caption-flag">Caption - not for posting</div>
    <div class="caption-body">For a sensitive child, a strong smell can be as overwhelming as a loud noise, and there is a neurological reason it hits so fast.<br><br>Of all the senses, smell has an unusually direct route to the brain's emotion and memory centers. It largely skips the relay station the other senses pass through. So a powerful scent can set off an intense emotional reaction before a child has any chance to think it through or put it into words.<br><br>The cafeteria at lunch, someone's strong perfume, a cleaning spray, any of it can tip the balance almost instantly. That is not pickiness or being difficult on purpose. It is a sense wired straight into the feeling brain. The nose really does have a direct line to emotion.</div>
