  <div class="slide caption-page" data-document-role="page" data-label="C9 - Caption (copy/paste)">
    <div class="caption-flag">Caption - not for posting</div>
    <div class="caption-body">Sitting still can be genuinely harder for some children than we give them credit for, and it comes down to how the brain uses movement.<br><br>The systems that sense movement and body position feed directly into the brain's networks for attention and arousal. For some nervous systems, movement is organizing. It actually helps the brain settle and focus. So the child who cannot seem to stop wiggling may be seeking the exact input that helps them concentrate and stay regulated.<br><br>When we force stillness, we can accidentally take away the very tool their brain is reaching for. The fidgeting is not defiance or a lack of discipline. For a lot of kids, it is the brain looking for regulation through motion. Some kids genuinely focus better when they are moving.</div>
    <div class="caption-tags">#movement #vestibular #proprioception</div>
