  <div class="slide caption-page" data-document-role="page" data-label="C10 - Caption (copy/paste)">
    <div class="caption-flag">Caption - not for posting</div>
    <div class="caption-body">When your sensitive child refuses a food, their brain is often reacting to texture, not taste.<br><br>Touch sensitivity does not stop at the skin. The mouth is densely wired for touch, so tactile over-responsivity shows up there too. A food can get a hard no because of how it feels rather than how it tastes, and that reaction is genuinely real in the brain. Mushy, slimy, or mixed textures can register as unpleasant, even threatening, in a way that is hard to talk a child out of.<br><br>So that is not always a picky phase to push through. Sometimes it is real sensory information coming from a system doing its job a little too well. When we listen to the texture, the refusal starts to make a lot more sense. Texture is the whole story.</div>
